            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The totality of the earth, representing all four points of the compass (North, South, East, West) and signifying global or universal scope.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Revelation 7:1\",\"Matthew 24:31\",\"Mark 13:27\",\"Daniel 7:2\",\"Daniel 8:8\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Angelic beings or spiritual agencies carrying out God's will globally.\",\"basis\":[\"Zechariah 6:5\",\"Revelation 7:1\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Divine judgment or chaotic, destructive forces of nature that God restrains or unleashes upon the earth.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 7:1\",\"Jeremiah 49:36\",\"Daniel 7:2\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The pervasive, life-giving power of the Holy Spirit or divine breath.\",\"basis\":[\"Ezekiel 37:9\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The 'four winds' is a well-established ancient idiom for the four points of the compass, denoting the entire world. While the exact function of the winds can vary contextually (e.g., gathering the elect, churning the sea, bringing breath), the spatial scope they represent is consistently universal.\"}",
